{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And you shall demolish their altars.</b> The verse returns to it because of <b>and you shall destroy their name.</b> <b>And their asherim you shall burn with fire.</b> And in the parashah of Va'etchanan it is written 'ואשריהם תגדעון' (and their asherim you shall cut down). And our Rabbis said: when they planted and afterwards worshipped — he cuts down what was added after it was worshipped; and when they planted after the worship — all of it is by burning." "<b>And the carved images of their Elohim you shall cut down.</b> And in the parashah of Va'etchanan it is written 'ופסילי אלהיהם תשרפון באש' (and the carved images of their Elohim you shall burn with fire). To say: a tree that they carved while attached and then cut down — he takes what he cut down, this is the meaning of תגדעון (you shall cut down); but a carved image that was entirely carved while detached — it is burned. Another interpretation: to give here and there both cutting and burning, for if he burns without cutting, perhaps he will not burn it all; and if he cuts without burning, he might re-erect it." "<b>And their asherim you shall burn with fire.</b> Because the land of Canaan was secured for Israel from HaShem, and when they said regarding the calf 'these are your Elohim, O Israel,' they desired many divinities, and all idolatry in the land of Canaan became Israel's property; and idolatry that belongs to Israel has no permanent nullification." "<b>And you shall destroy their name.</b> As we find 'their name' is used in construct state."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:52:34.298646204Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}